{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Configuring an IAM Identity Center instance for an Amazon Q Business application","context":"## Creating a cross-region integration\n\nAmazon Q Business, including Amazon Q Apps, can also integrate with IAM Identity Center in\nany commercial region\nwhere IAM Identity Center is available (excluding opt-in and special regions), even if\nthat region isn’t one of the regions supported by Amazon Q Business. You\ncan choose to create a cross-region integration if you already have an IAM Identity Center\ninstance configured in a region that Amazon Q Business isn’t currently\navailable in.\n\nWhen you create a cross-region Amazon Q Business and IAM Identity Center-integration, you\nenable Amazon Q Business to make cross-region calls in order to access and\nstore information from your IAM Identity Center instance, such as user and group attributes. This\nfunctionality allows Amazon Q Business to support IAM Identity Center-enabled applications\nin regions different from where your IAM Identity Center instance is ingested. When you create a\ncross-region integration, your Amazon Q Business application will have access\nto user and group information from an IAM Identity Center instance deployed in a different region.\nIn these cross-region calls, Amazon Q Business might send the following user\nattributes:\n\n* Email address\n* Account in AWS Organizations\n* User ID\n* Group name\n* Group ID\n\n**Important:**\n\nAmazon Q Business supports cross-region IAM Identity Center and Amazon Q Business integrations only for organization level instances. Amazon Q Business\ndoesn't support cross-region IAM Identity Center integrations for account level instances. For\nmore information on IAM Identity Center instance types and their use cases, see Understanding types of IAM Identity Center instances.\n\nIf you create a cross-region integration between an Amazon Q Business\napplication and an IAM Identity Center instance, you may experience higher latency when using\nAmazon Q Business due to the increased overhead of making cross-region\ncalls. The increase in latency will be proportional to the distance of the Amazon Q Business region from the IAM Identity Center region you're using. We recommend\nperforming latency tests for your specific user case. We don't recommend using this\nfeature if you have more than 100 groups per user in your IAM Identity Center instance.\n\nWhen you create a cross-region IAM Identity Center and Amazon Q Business integration, any\nAmazon Q Business indices associated with your application are billed in\nthe Amazon Q Business region they're created in. User subscriptions for an\nAmazon Q Business application using a cross-region IAM Identity Center integration are\nbilled in the region the IAM Identity Center instance is created in. For more information on\n\n*[truncated — additional content omitted]*","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazonq/latest/qbusiness-ug/idc-setup.html"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"How do I troubleshoot errors that I receive when I set up Amazon Q Business with IAM Identity Center?","context":"### There are missing identity-aware session settings\n\nIdentity-aware sessions are required for Amazon Q Business to authenticate users, call APIs, and perform user-specific operations. If identity-aware sessions aren't activated in your organization's management account, then you might receive the following error:\n\n\"Contact your administrator in order to enable Amazon Q in the AWS Console. You must ensure identity-aware sessions are enabled in the AWS Orgs Management account.\"\n\nTo activate identity-aware sessions, complete the following steps:\n\n1. [Activate an organization instance of IAM Identity Center](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/enable-identity-center.html#to-enable-identity-center-instance). \n **Note:** If you use an AWS cross-Region IAM Identity Center setup, then make sure that your organization instance supports cross-Region connections.\n2. [Activate identity-aware sessions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/identity-aware-sessions.html#enable-identity-aware-sessions-q).\n3. Confirm your [Region availability](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/general/latest/gr/amazonq.html#qbusiness_region) for Amazon Q Business.\n\nRelated information\n-------------------\n\n[AWS IAM Identity Center](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/what-is.html)\n\n[Organization and account instances of IAM Identity Center](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/identity-center-instances.html)","url":"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/qbusiness-identity-center-setup-errors"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Deploy AWS applications and access AWS accounts across multiple Regions with IAM Identity Center | AWS Security Blog","context":"### **What happens when you try to enable your application in a Region where Identity Center isn’t configured?**\n\nIf Frankfurt (eu-central-1) hasn’t been added to your Identity Center instance, the application console will detect your organization instance in N. Virginia (us-east-1), and prompt you to enable Frankfurt (eu-central-1) first.\n\nFigure 8: AWS Deadline cloud console wizard when Identity Center isn’t configured in the current Region\n\n> **Note**: Existing deployments of AWS managed applications that use cross-Region calls with Identity Center (for example, Amazon Q Business) continue to function normally. When deploying an AWS managed application that supports cross-Region calls, we recommend configuring it to use Identity Center in the same Region, provided the prerequisites are met. Otherwise, you can configure the application to use Identity Center from one of its enabled Regions. See the respective AWS application’s User Guide to learn if it supports cross-Region calls to Identity Center","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/deploy-aws-applications-and-access-aws-accounts-across-multiple-regions-with-iam-identity-center/"}]}}

## Recent AWS announcements (What's New)

Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS IAM Identity Center extends multi-Region support to more AWS Regions - AWS","context":"AWS IAM Identity Center, a service that connects workforce identities to AWS and streamlines access management, now supports multi-Region replication in additional AWS Regions, including those in AWS GovCloud (US) and AWS China. Previously, multi-Region support was available only in commercial AWS Regions. This feature enhances the resilience of user access to AWS accounts and applications by automatically replicating identities, entitlements, and other information from the primary Region to additional Regions. Users can continue to access their AWS accounts using already provisioned entitlements in the additional Regions during disruptions in the primary Region. Multi-Region support is available for organization instances using an external identity provider or the IAM Identity Center directory as the identity source and requires a multi-Region customer managed KMS key. To enable multi-Region support, create a new instance or configure an existing instance with a multi-Region CMK in AWS KMS. Standard AWS KMS charges apply for storing and using CMKs, while IAM Identity Center remains free. Multi-Region support is available in various AWS Regions. For more information, visit the Using IAM Identity Center across multiple AWS Regions page and the list of AWS applications that support deployment in additional Regions","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/aws-iam-identity-center-extends-multi-region-support-to-more-aws-regions/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS IAM Identity Center now supports cross-Region trusted identity propagation - AWS","context":"AWS IAM Identity Center now supports cross-Region trusted identity propagation, allowing users' identity context to be carried with their requests to AWS services in different AWS Regions. This feature provides greater flexibility in architecting and deploying workloads across Regions without compromising fine-grained access control or auditing capabilities. Previously, trusted identity propagation was limited to services within the same Region, requiring consolidation of services or broad IAM roles for access to services in other Regions. Cross-Region trusted identity propagation is available at no additional cost in all AWS Regions where IAM Identity Center is available. This feature enables granting access based on user identity and underlying group associations, while maintaining individual user access control and auditing capabilities","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/aws-iam-identity-center-cross-region-tip/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS IAM Identity Center enables account access and application use in multiple AWS Regions - AWS","context":"AWS IAM Identity Center is a service that helps manage access to AWS accounts and applications for workforces. It is now possible to replicate IAM Identity Center from the primary AWS Region to additional Regions of choice, enhancing user access resilience and enabling deployment of AWS applications in preferred Regions. This feature automatically replicates identities, entitlements, and other information from the primary Region to additional Regions. IAM Identity Center users can continue to access their AWS accounts using the already provisioned entitlements in the additional Regions during disruptions in the primary Region. AWS application administrators can deploy applications in additional Regions using the standard application deployment workflow and assign users to the application in that Region. IAM Identity Center multi-Region support is available in 17 commercial AWS Regions for organization instances connected to an external identity provider, such as Okta, and requires a multi-Region customer managed KMS key. Standard AWS KMS charges apply for storing and using CMKs. IAM Identity Center is provided at no additional cost. To learn more and get started, visit the IAM Identity Center product detail page and user guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/02/aws-iam-identity-center-multi-region-aws-account-access-and-application-deployment/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"AWS IAM Identity Center supports one-click multi-Region option for new organization instances - AWS","context":"AWS IAM Identity Center has introduced a one-click option for enabling multi-Region support when creating a new organization instance. Previously, this process involved several steps such as creating a customer managed KMS key, configuring key policies, and manually adding Regions. Now, customers can choose from three instance configuration options: single-Region instance, multi-Region instance, or custom instance. The multi-Region instance option automatically creates a customer managed multi-Region KMS key and replicates the instance to an additional Region, ensuring resilient AWS account and application access. This feature is available in 17 commercial AWS Regions for organization instances of IAM Identity Center, with standard AWS KMS charges applying for the customer managed key. To get started, refer to the IAM Identity Center User Guide and the Using IAM Identity Center across multiple AWS Regions page. IAM Identity Center remains free of charge","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/08/aws-iam-identity-center-supports-one-click-multi-region-option-new-organization-instances/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"AWS IAM Identity Center extends multi-Region support to Identity Center directory - AWS","context":"AWS IAM Identity Center now supports replicating instances that use the Identity Center directory to manage and authenticate their workforce to additional AWS Regions. This feature, which extends multi-Region support, allows for increased resilience and deployment flexibility. When enabled, IAM Identity Center automatically replicates identities, entitlements, and other information from the primary Region to additional Regions. IAM Identity Center multi-Region support is available in 17 commercial AWS Regions for organization instances of IAM Identity Center that are configured with a multi-Region customer managed KMS key. AWS application administrators can deploy applications in additional Regions using the standard application deployment workflow while IAM Identity Center is managed in the primary Region. This feature is provided at no additional cost, but standard AWS KMS charges apply for storing and using CMKs. To learn more about IAM Identity Center and get started, visit the product detail page and IAM Identity Center User Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/aws-iam-identity-center-extends-multi-region-support-to-identity-center-directory/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify AWS docs on Q Business and IAM Identity Center region requirements."}

